body:
Integrates the complete SCAN production-readiness stack onto `main` and closes the staged draft-PR lineage.

Included roadmap:

coverage/gaps → uncertainty challenger → triage ranking → layered provenance → parity scenarios/distribution → broader calibration → independent reconstruction proof → authorized runtime validation → operational hardening → v1.0 certification.

This integration also closes repository-level release loose ends:
- root and `Anchor/` documentation now point to SCAN v1.0 instead of the stale v0.23/v0.27 checkpoint;
- the v1.0 release index and release notes are present;
- the final certification workflow now reruns on `main`;
- the main release commit must be a two-parent integration merge whose tree is byte-identical to this checked source head;
- the main release gate verifies successful broader-calibration and independent-reconstruction stage checks on that source head;
- after all main-side certification gates pass, the workflow publishes the immutable `v1.0.0` GitHub release and refuses to move an existing tag to different bytes.

The earlier v1.0 candidate at `7b63b19932fd4c20c7599c58959202527d5dd3f0` passed 210 tests, the 9-lane Linux/macOS/Windows × Python 3.11/3.12/3.13 matrix, and live authorized runtime validation. Subsequent repository/documentation/publication cleanup changed the source-head identity, so the current PR head is being fully recertified rather than inheriting that older certificate.

After merge, `main` is recertified again before publication. The final GitHub release is therefore bound to the actual merge commit and sealed package bytes, not merely to the pre-merge branch head.

Authority boundaries and non-claims documented in the v1.0 certification contract remain unchanged.

Final production-readiness stage, stacked on PR #57.

This tranche does not add another semantic interpretation layer. It turns the accumulated SCAN work into a commit- and byte-specific release certification process.

Adds:
- SCAN engine version 1.0.0 candidate;
- stronger packaged qualification requiring the current coverage/gap, uncertainty, triage, and layered-provenance projections;
- expanded LLM credential stripping during mechanical qualification;
- `scan-body release-certify` local exact-package certification;
- `scan-body verify-release-certification`;
- sealed local certification manifest with package-manifest and qualification hashes;
- explicit separation between local mechanical certification and CI-only release evidence;
- final certification contract documentation;
- Linux/macOS/Windows matrix on Python 3.11/3.12/3.13;
- complete inherited regression suite;
- live Linux network-denied authorized-runtime validation;
- wrong-plan-hash fail-closed proof;
- clean wheel build/install and installed-CLI scan smoke;
- deterministic source archive;
- aggregate `V1_CI_RELEASE_CERTIFICATE.json` and `V1_CI_MANIFEST.json` emitted only after all prerequisite gates succeed.

Authority boundaries remain explicit:
- ordinary SCAN stays static/non-executing;
- runtime evidence remains explicitly authorized and sidecar-only;
- local package PASS is not allowed to self-promote into CI-wide certification;
- certification applies only to the exact commit/package bytes tested;
- no universal semantic completeness, runtime equivalence, hostile-code sandbox, distributed locking, exhaustive fuzzing, or production SLA is implied.

This is the final stage in:
coverage/gaps → uncertainty challenger → triage ranking → layered provenance → parity scenarios/distribution → broader calibration → independent reconstruction proof → authorized runtime validation → operational hardening → v1.0 certification.

Ninth production-readiness stage, stacked on PR #56.

Scope is deliberately limited to **operational hardening** before final v1.0 certification.

Adds:
- exclusive scanner-owned output leases for canonical scan writers;
- live-writer contention refusal;
- stale same-host dead-PID lease recovery;
- fail-closed handling for unreadable/foreign-host leases;
- output-root symlink rejection;
- exception-safe lease cleanup around scan and scan-manifest;
- malformed/non-UTF-8 regression corpus;
- catastrophic MemoryError propagation check;
- manifest path-traversal refusal before output creation;
- bounded synthetic large-repository degradation checks;
- focused Linux/macOS/Windows hardening matrix on Python 3.11 and 3.13;
- clean wheel/console-entry-point smoke validation.

Inherited regressions continue to cover parser/adaptor isolation, symlink containment, resource ceilings, cancellation, resume-after-budget, corrupt SQLite quarantine/rebuild, deterministic projections, package integrity, and release-query acceptance.

Authority boundary:
- operational metadata does not become canonical evidence;
- ordinary SCAN remains static/non-executing;
- runtime validation remains explicitly authorized and sidecar-only;
- resource stops remain coverage statements rather than success claims;
- catastrophic process failures are not reclassified as parser uncertainty.

Explicit non-claims:
- no distributed or multi-host writer coordination;
- no network-filesystem locking guarantee;
- no universal power-loss atomicity;
- no exhaustive parser fuzz proof;
- no production performance SLA.

Final v1.0 certification remains the next independent roadmap stage.

Eighth production-readiness stage, stacked on PR #55.

Scope is deliberately limited to **runtime validation where explicitly authorized**.

Adds:
- `scan-runtime-validation-plan/0.1`;
- `scan-authorized-runtime-validation/0.1`;
- `scan-body runtime-validate TARGET PLAN --out DIR --authorize-plan-sha256 SHA`;
- exact plan-hash authorization before any execution;
- temporary-copy execution;
- shell disabled;
- sanitized environment;
- Linux network namespace with loopback only;
- timeout and bounded recorded output;
- safe relative-path assertions;
- source-tree hash verification before/after execution;
- runtime assertions for exit code, stdout/stderr, files, JSON probe values, hashes, and duration.

Authority boundary:
- ordinary SCAN remains static/read-only/non-executing;
- runtime results are sidecar observations only;
- no scan_index.sqlite writes;
- no automatic semantic overlay;
- no static coverage upgrade;
- no automatic reconstruction-anchor promotion;
- a runtime PASS means only the exact authorized assertions passed in that run.

The CI gate proves state transition, persistence, a dynamically emitted probe value, elapsed time, secret-env non-forwarding, source immutability, wrong-hash refusal, and negative-observation preservation.

Operational hardening remains the next independent roadmap stage.

Seventh production-readiness stage, stacked on PR #54.

Scope is **independent reconstruction proof**.

Adds:
- `scan-independent-worker-receipt/0.1` verification;
- `scan-independent-reconstruction-proof/0.1` lineage/adjudication report;
- `scan-body verify-independent-reconstruction ...`;
- challenge-manifest leak/tamper checks;
- candidate tree hash binding;
- submission hash binding;
- enforced-isolation requirement (declared-only network isolation is rejected);
- final challenge -> worker -> candidate -> private trial lineage verification.

CI qualification uses three separate jobs:
1. preparation creates a private source certification, public source-free challenge, and separate private evaluator;
2. worker job performs **no repository checkout**, downloads **only** the public challenge, and generates the candidate inside a Linux network namespace;
3. adjudication downloads the private evaluator and worker output, performs a fresh SCAN, scores the candidate, and emits the independent proof.

The worker is deliberately a deterministic reference worker. This proves the isolation/handoff/scoring machinery without external model secrets or services. It is explicitly **not** claimed as an external-LLM benchmark.

Runtime/visual/timing equivalence remains out of scope and is the next roadmap stage.

Sixth production-readiness stage, stacked on PR #53.

This PR is **calibration-only** with respect to scanner behavior. It does not add adapters, extraction rules, runtime execution, or promotion paths.

Calibration matrix:
- frozen NotepadNext — Qt/C++;
- frozen Moji — TypeScript/Electron;
- frozen pell — plain JavaScript/web;
- unsupported Python CLI fixture;
- mixed-language C++/HTML/TypeScript/Python fixture;
- incomplete metadata-only acquisition;
- ambiguous static web routing;
- forced resource-budget interruption.

The gate runs duplicate cold scans plus the full downstream chain and checks:
- deterministic canonical rows/projections;
- zero integrity ERRORs;
- gaps/challenger/triage count consistency;
- layered-provenance reconciliation;
- Blueprint/parity 1:1 contract derivation;
- portable parity regeneration;
- unsupported CLI does not invent GUI surfaces;
- incomplete acquisition remains externally blocked;
- resource limits remain explicit gaps;
- ambiguous routing remains uncertain/advisory;
- scanner runtime contains no calibration specimen-name branches.

Observed counts are recorded but not hand-tuned as pass criteria, avoiding calibration overfitting.

Independent reconstruction proof remains the next roadmap stage.

Fifth production-readiness upgrade, stacked on PR #52.

Scope is deliberately limited to **parity scenarios/distribution**.

Adds:
- `parity_scenarios.json` and `parity_scenarios.feature` derived from the Blueprint's embedded conformance manifest;
- exactly one static parity scenario for each MAPPED + REQUIRED contract;
- no scenarios generated from PARTIAL/BLOCKED/UNKNOWN/advisory observations;
- explicit static-rescan execution class and no runtime/visual/timing equivalence claim;
- portable regeneration using only the Blueprint, with no original source or SQLite DB required;
- compact `AGENTS.md` discovery pointer with Blueprint hash and verification command;
- protection for existing project-authored `AGENTS.md`: SCAN never overwrites it.

Authority boundary:
- embedded Blueprint conformance manifest remains authoritative;
- parity scenarios are derived projections, not an independent specification;
- candidate self-report has no authority;
- AGENTS.md is discovery metadata only;
- runtime parity remains deferred to the later authorized runtime-validation stage.

Broader calibration remains the next independent roadmap stage.

Fourth production-readiness upgrade, stacked on PR #51.

Scope is deliberately limited to **layered provenance**.

Adds:
- automatic `layered_provenance.json/md`;
- explicit `scan-body provenance-layers DB --out-dir DIR` regeneration command;
- E0 source/evidence, E1 mechanical anatomy, E2 semantic interpretation, E3 reconstruction contract, E4 human description layers;
- per-object coverage preserved exactly from the canonical semantic graph;
- typed backward proof traversal to evidence/source IDs;
- immediate and lower-layer support IDs;
- contradiction relation visibility;
- explicit `UNCLASSIFIED` handling for future object types;
- refresh after semantic-overlay ingestion and reconstruction promotion so E2/E3 projections cannot go stale.

Authority boundary:
- `scan_index.sqlite` remains the single canonical graph;
- no duplicate provenance database;
- no confidence aggregation;
- no promotion;
- E4 is presentation-only and cannot upgrade lower-layer evidence.

Parity scenarios/distribution remains the next independent roadmap stage.

Third production-readiness upgrade, stacked on PR #50.

Scope is deliberately limited to **triage ranking**.

Adds:
- automatic `triage_ranking.json/md` after coverage/gaps + challenger;
- explicit `scan-body triage-gaps DB --out-dir DIR` regeneration command;
- transparent 0..100 investigation score composed from:
  - structural connectivity (20 max),
  - actionable human-surface reach (30 max),
  - effect/state/NEST/feedback reach (20 max),
  - reconstruction-anchor dependency (20 max),
  - challenger mechanical-recheck support (10 max);
- raw signal counts and component contributions emitted for every ranked gap;
- deterministic tie-breaking by gap ID;
- high-degree connectivity capped so generic hubs cannot dominate.

Authority boundary:
- ranking means investigation leverage only, not truth;
- no confidence effect;
- no promotion effect;
- no canonical writes;
- coverage state and gap category are not score weights;
- no LLM dependency.

Layered provenance is intentionally the next independent roadmap stage.

Second production-readiness upgrade, stacked on PR #49.

Scope is deliberately limited to the **uncertainty challenger** stage.

Adds:
- automatic `uncertainty_challenges.json/md` after coverage/gaps generation;
- explicit `scan-body challenge-uncertainty DB --out-dir DIR` regeneration command;
- deterministic challenge types for acquisition, parser, graph, surface-route, effect-route, completeness, and recorded-finding gaps;
- one-hop candidate evidence/neighbor discovery from the existing canonical graph;
- named mechanical recheck proposals;
- explicit dispositions for external-input blockers, scanner-work requirements, available mechanical rechecks, and no-local-support cases.

Authority boundary:
- no canonical writes;
- no evidence promotion;
- no new state proposal;
- no scalar confidence;
- no rank/priority/score;
- no LLM dependency;
- any state change still requires deterministic rescan or an existing promotion gate.

The next roadmap stage, triage ranking, is intentionally out of scope.

First production-readiness upgrade after released v0.28.0.

Scope is deliberately limited to the first roadmap step: **coverage/gaps**.

Adds:
- automatic `coverage_report.json/md` and `gaps.json/md` on ordinary scans;
- explicit `scan-body coverage-gaps DB --out-dir DIR` regeneration command;
- exact coverage-state rollups across canonical files/nodes/edges/findings/semantic graph/completeness;
- surface and effect closure summaries;
- unresolved acquisition/parser/node/edge/finding/completeness/surface/effect gap records with canonical IDs and evidence IDs;
- no scalar confidence score;
- no priority/ranking;
- no evidence promotion or canonical writes;
- deterministic/read-only invariants and reconciliation tests.

v0.28 remains untouched and sealed. v0.29 intentionally has no package manifest yet.

Next roadmap stages (uncertainty challenger, triage ranking, layered provenance, parity scenarios, broader calibration, reconstruction/runtime/hardening) are explicitly out of scope for this PR.

Release-only correction after the v0.28.0 merge.

The first publication attempt failed closed because the sdist archive did not reproduce its earlier byte hash. No release/tag was created.

This PR does not modify `Anchor/engine/v0.28`.

It:
- removes the non-reproducible sdist from published release assets;
- builds the source ZIP twice from pristine copies and requires byte identity;
- builds the wheel twice and retains the sealed wheel SHA-256 check;
- publishes `PACKAGE_MANIFEST.json` and `SHA256SUMS.txt`;
- updates release notes to state exactly what is sealed and published.

After merge to `main`, the existing publish workflow will create tag/release `v0.28.0` only if all deterministic artifact checks pass.
